  <h2>What Makes a Custom Tiny Home Different</h2>
  <p>A custom tiny home is a permanent house, not a product off a lot. It sits on a real foundation, connects to real utilities and is permitted like any other single-family home. What makes it "tiny" is the footprint — anywhere from about 400 to 1,800 square feet — and the discipline it takes to make every one of those square feet work hard.</p>
  <p>Cowboy Tiny Homes designs custom tiny homes in Texas from a blank sheet. We start with your lot, your must-haves and your budget, then draw a home that fits all three. You are not choosing between three floor plans in a catalog; you are deciding where the morning sun lands on your kitchen table.</p>
  <p>Because these homes are built to the International Residential Code (IRC) and your local zoning, they can be financed with conventional mortgages, insured as a primary residence and sold like any other house. That is the biggest difference between a custom tiny home on a foundation and a <a href="/tiny-homes-on-wheels-texas/">tiny home on wheels</a>.</p>

  <h2>Tiny, Small and Large Single-Family Homes</h2>
  <p>"Tiny" means different things to different people, so we design across the range:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Home size</th><th>Typical footprint</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tiny house</td><td>400–600 sq ft</td><td>Singles, retirees, weekend places, downsizers who want one-level living</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small house</td><td>600–1,100 sq ft</td><td>Couples, remote workers who need an office, small families</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Right-sized home</td><td>1,100–1,800 sq ft</td><td>Families who want efficiency without giving up a second bedroom or a guest room</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Not sure how much space you need? The <a href="/tools/tiny-house-size-planner/">tiny house size planner</a> adds up the rooms you want and tells you which home types fit.</p>

  <h2>Built to Code: IRC, Appendix Q and Local Zoning</h2>
  <p>Every custom home we design is drawn to the IRC, the model code most Texas cities use for one- and two-family homes. For very small homes — 400 square feet or less — some jurisdictions have adopted <strong>IRC Appendix Q</strong>, which relaxes rules for ceiling heights in lofts, stair and ladder design and emergency egress from sleeping lofts. Appendix Q only applies where a city has adopted it, so we confirm what your jurisdiction uses during the feasibility study.</p>
  <p>Zoning is the other half. Many Texas cities set a minimum house size, minimum lot width or design standards for new homes, and HOA deed restrictions can add their own square-footage minimums. Outside city limits the rules are usually lighter, but counties still regulate septic systems, floodplains and driveway access. Our <a href="/guides/tiny-house-laws-texas/">guide to tiny house laws in Texas</a> walks through all of it.</p>

  <h2>What Your Custom Tiny Home Design Includes</h2>
  <p>Every Cowboy Tiny Homes project comes with the full design package, so permitting and construction start from a complete set of drawings:</p>
  <ul class="checklist checklist--2">
    <li>Feasibility study</li><li>Floor plans</li><li>Exterior elevations</li><li>Roof plan</li>
    <li>Site plan creation</li><li>Electrical layout</li><li>Exterior and interior renderings</li><li>3D perspective renderings</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Design Ideas That Make Small Homes Live Large</h2>
  <h3>Plan for Texas heat first</h3>
  <p>Deep porches and roof overhangs on the south and west, a light-colored or metal roof, spray-foam or well-sealed attic insulation and a right-sized heat pump keep a small home comfortable and cheap to run through a Texas August.</p>
  <h3>Borrow space you already have</h3>
  <p>Vaulted ceilings, tall windows, sliding glass to a covered patio and sightlines that run the full length of the house make 700 square feet feel like 1,000. Outdoor living areas are cheap square footage in Texas, where you can use them most of the year.</p>
  <h3>Build storage into the bones</h3>
  <p>Stair drawers, window seats with lift-up lids, a pantry wall instead of upper cabinets and a laundry closet that doubles as a mudroom keep clutter from taking over.</p>
  <h3>Choose a style, not a compromise</h3>
  <p>Modern farmhouse, Hill Country limestone and metal, Texas ranch with board-and-batten, or clean contemporary — small homes can carry any style. See examples on our <a href="/home-designs/">house designs</a> page, including the two-story <a href="/gonzales-modern-living/">Gonzales Modern Living</a> plan.</p>

  <h2>How Much Does a Custom Tiny Home Cost in Texas?</h2>
  <p>For planning, builder-built custom homes on a foundation in Texas typically run about <strong>$175 to $320 per finished square foot</strong> at standard finishes. Basic finishes can bring that down roughly 15%; premium finishes can push it up 30% or more. That puts a 600-square-foot tiny house at roughly $105,000–$192,000 and a 1,000-square-foot small home at roughly $175,000–$320,000 before land.</p>
  <p>Land, utility connections, septic or well, driveways and site work are separate and vary widely by property. For your own numbers, try the <a href="/tools/tiny-home-cost-calculator/">tiny home cost calculator</a>, read the full <a href="/guides/cost-to-build-a-tiny-house-in-texas/">cost to build a tiny house in Texas</a> breakdown, or compare payment options in the <a href="/tools/tiny-home-loan-calculator/">loan calculator</a>.</p>

  <h2>Custom Tiny Home vs Buying a Prebuilt Model</h2>
  <p>Factory-built tiny homes can be a good value when a stock floor plan happens to fit your lot and your life. The trade-off is flexibility. A prebuilt model is designed for an average buyer and an average site, so you adapt to it. A custom tiny home is designed for one buyer and one site, so it adapts to you — window placement for your view, a kitchen sized for how you cook, a porch facing away from the afternoon sun.</p>
  <p>Custom also helps with permitting. Because the drawings are prepared for your jurisdiction from the start, there is less back-and-forth with plan reviewers about setbacks, egress, energy code or foundation details. And because you choose every finish, you control where the money goes: splurge on the kitchen, save on the guest bath.</p>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li><strong>Choose custom</strong> if your lot is irregular, you want a specific style, or you plan to live in the home full-time for years.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider a stock plan</strong> if your budget is tight and a standard layout already fits — we can adapt one of our <a href="/home-designs/">house designs</a> as a middle ground.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where We Build Custom Tiny Homes</h2>
  <p>Cowboy Tiny Homes works with homeowners across Texas, including <a href="/tiny-home-builders-san-antonio/">San Antonio</a>, <a href="/tiny-home-builders-austin/">Austin</a>, <a href="/tiny-home-builders-dallas/">Dallas</a>, <a href="/tiny-home-builders-fort-worth/">Fort Worth</a> and <a href="/tiny-home-builders-houston/">Houston</a>, plus rural acreage and Hill Country land. Each city handles permits a little differently, and we plan for that from day one.</p>
